# Exploring multivariate wine data

This collection ports the complete analysis sequence in [Using R for Multivariate Analysis](https://little-book-of-r-for-multivariate-analysis.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/multivariateanalysis.html). The source uses the UCI Wine data: chemical measurements from 178 wines grown in one Italian region and derived from three cultivars. These are real observations, not simulated data.

The source reads the comma-separated file without a header. BioLang does the same, then renames the fourteen columns by position to the source's stable `V1` through `V14` names. This preserves every source byte and avoids runtime-specific names for headerless columns.

```biolang
fn read_source_wine(path) {
    let data = read_csv(path, {header: false})
    let names = colnames(data)
    data = rename(data, names[0], "V1")
    data = rename(data, names[1], "V2")
    data = rename(data, names[2], "V3")
    data = rename(data, names[3], "V4")
    data = rename(data, names[4], "V5")
    data = rename(data, names[5], "V6")
    data = rename(data, names[6], "V7")
    data = rename(data, names[7], "V8")
    data = rename(data, names[8], "V9")
    data = rename(data, names[9], "V10")
    data = rename(data, names[10], "V11")
    data = rename(data, names[11], "V12")
    data = rename(data, names[12], "V13")
    rename(data, names[13], "V14")
}

let wine = read_source_wine("data/wine.data")
{
    samples: nrow(wine),
    columns: ncol(wine),
    first_five_samples: wine |> head(5)
}
```

```biolang
let cultivar_1 = wine |> filter(|row| row.V1 == 1)
let cultivar_2 = wine |> filter(|row| row.V1 == 2)
let cultivar_3 = wine |> filter(|row| row.V1 == 3)
table({
    cultivar: [1, 2, 3],
    samples: [nrow(cultivar_1), nrow(cultivar_2), nrow(cultivar_3)]
})
```

The exact group sizes are 59, 71, and 48.

## Matrix scatterplot

The source first plots the first five chemical measurements as a scatterplot matrix. BioLang constructs all 25 panels from `wine`: density curves on the diagonal and every ordered pairwise scatterplot off the diagonal.

```biolang
fn matrix_panel(data, x_variable, y_variable) {
    if x_variable == y_variable {
        density_plot(data |> col(x_variable), {
            title: x_variable, xlabel: "", ylabel: "",
            width: 260, height: 220
        })
    } else {
        plot(data |> select(x_variable, y_variable), {
            type: "scatter", x: x_variable, y: y_variable,
            xlabel: "", ylabel: "", width: 260, height: 220
        })
    }
}

let matrix_variables = ["V2", "V3", "V4", "V5", "V6"]
let matrix_panels = matrix_variables |> flat_map(|y_variable|
    matrix_variables |> map(|x_variable| matrix_panel(wine, x_variable, y_variable)))
plot_grid(matrix_panels, {
    columns: 5,
    panel_labels: ["", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""],
    title: "Scatterplot matrix: V2 through V6",
    width: 1250, height: 1060, gap: 6,
    panel_width: 230, panel_height: 185
})
```

## One pair in detail

It next plots V4 against V5, first as one point cloud and then with cultivar encoded by colour and identified in the legend.

```biolang
plot(wine |> select("V4", "V5"), {
    type: "scatter", x: "V4", y: "V5",
    title: "V4 versus V5", xlabel: "V4", ylabel: "V5",
    theme: "publication", width: 700, height: 500
})
```

```biolang
stats_relationship_plot(wine |> col("V4"), wine |> col("V5"), {
    group: wine |> col("V1"), group_order: ["1", "2", "3"],
    fit: false, note: false, legend_title: "Cultivar",
    title: "V4 versus V5 by cultivar", x_label: "V4", y_label: "V5",
    width: 760, height: 520
})
```

The cultivar-identified figure shows that cultivar 2 tends to have lower V4 values than cultivar 1.

## Profile plot

Finally, the source puts V2 through V6 on one profile plot. Their different scales are immediately visible, especially the much larger mean and standard deviation of V6.

```biolang
let profiles = table({
    sample: range(1, nrow(wine) + 1),
    V2: wine |> col("V2"), V3: wine |> col("V3"),
    V4: wine |> col("V4"), V5: wine |> col("V5"),
    V6: wine |> col("V6")
})
plot(profiles, {
    type: "line", x: "sample", y: ["V2", "V3", "V4", "V5", "V6"],
    title: "Profiles of V2 through V6", xlabel: "Sample index", ylabel: "Value",
    theme: "publication", width: 900, height: 560
})
```

```biolang
assert nrow(wine) == 178, "exact UCI source rows"
assert ncol(wine) == 14, "cultivar plus thirteen chemical measurements"
assert nrow(cultivar_1) == 59 && nrow(cultivar_2) == 71 && nrow(cultivar_3) == 48, "exact cultivar counts"
assert wine[0].V1 == 1 && abs(wine[0].V2 - 14.23) < 0.000000000001, "exact first source row"
assert wine[177].V1 == 3 && abs(wine[177].V14 - 560) < 0.000000000001, "exact last source row"
"Exact source data verified; four figures generated from it by BioLang."
```

